            },
            "reviewBody": "This is an excellent sauce. Easy &amp; better than store bought. If you don't have sake use extra mirin."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "NonnaTia"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I must say, living here in Hawaii, I have never heard of a Teriyaki Sauce without fresh ginger and garlic! The recipe we use is 1:1 soy sauce and sugar (or splenda if you prefer). Grate the ginger and garlic to taste (more garlic than ginger) and add a splash of mirin. Cook until dissolved. We marinate chicken, beef and even pork in this sauce and it is wonderful. Sesame seeds are often added as well.",
            "datePublished": "2010-11-30T04:23:21.453Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "TruthKid"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I had cooked a pre-marinated teriyaki pork tenderloin, and they always scream out a desire for a sauce, so I did a quick search and the simplicity of this convinced me. I've had a hard time finding an asian market where I live, but I did manage to find mirin at a local supermarket. Unfortunately, I can't seem to find sake. I simply substituted mirin for the 1/4 cup of sake, substituted honey for sugar, added about 1/4 cup finely chopped scallions, 1 tsp chili garlic sauce, a dash of coriander, ground ginger and pre-made five spice, and about a tsp of corn starch whisked into some reserved soy sauce. While the sauce began to bubble as the starch thickened things, I whisked in about 1/2 Tbsp of toasted sesame seed oil and some orange zest. Once it bubbled for about a minute and a half I removed from the heat and let it sit for another minute. The thickness was perfect, and the taste was absolutely amazing. Once I find some sake locally, I'll be sure to try it with just the four ingredients listed.",
            "datePublished": "2010-10-02T12:37:58.500Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Gail Cobile"
            },
            "reviewBody": "No sake but subbed rice wine vinegar and the sauce is excellent. I've used it as a marinade for chops and a basting sauce for chicken wings. I quadruple the recipe and keep it in the fridge in a pint jar. (It won't go bad, promise!) Have used it to season stir fry dishes and even drizzled some onto fried rice. DH is Asian and we both love it.",
            "datePublished": "2013-01-12T09:52:44.427Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "TrendyBran9671"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Grated 1/4 tsp. fresh ginger &amp; 2 garlic cloves smashed",
            "datePublished": "2025-04-03T01:09:01.382Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "DNYGARD"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a really good, simple base recipe you can play with. I added approx 1/2 tspn. minced ginger and a clove of pressed garlic to boost the flavour. I also added 2 Tbspn. fresh crushed pineapple to cut some of the saltiness.",
            "datePublished": "2006-10-17T22:37:26.973Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Patti M"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Really a great teriyaki sauce. My husband, who was raised Japanese , and is very picky about that kind of thing, loved it. It is a little on the salty side, so I think I'll used low sodium soy sauce next time. I did thicken it with corn starch-great tip, thanks for that one, and I used the sauce to make chicken teriyaki rice bowls. DELICIOUS!",
            "datePublished": "2008-07-22T10:29:25.440Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Royall Clark"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Making a marinate at 8:00 in the evening and what? No Teriyaki sauce in the house! Jumped to the AR search and found this wonderful recipe. Believe it or not I had both sake and the mirin in the fridge and had this whipped up in no time! Only change I made was I just zapped it in the microwave for about 20 seconds to warm up the sauce so the sugar would dissolve quicker. Nice flavor plus the price and availability was great! ;-)",
            "datePublished": "2011-10-23T00:10:16.010Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SoapyScoop6762"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I feel like anyone who thinks this recipe is too salty is not using actual dark soy sauce. Dark soy sauce is fermented longer and has less sodium content than low-sodium soy, is sweeter and has much more flavor! Adding additional ingredients, such as ginger, garlic and/or crushed red pepper flakes is not a bad idea for additional flavors either, but the base recipe is delicious when properly prepared.",
            "datePublished": "2024-08-05T18:50:52.917Z"
          },
          {
